// #88, review round 1: the reference rules hold in a GENERIC body, at each
// instantiation code generation emits, and `p == q` there is refused by name.
//
// A generic body is monomorphized, not walked by the type checker, so the
// operator rule (PD0083) never saw it: `p == q` over two borrows compared the
// two ADDRESSES. Measured on main (903ba02) and on round 1 alike: it compiled,
// ran and printed `ne` for `same(5, 5)` — while the non-generic twin
// (tests/reject/reference_operands_compared.pd) is refused. Each body is now
// checked at its instantiations, and the refusal says which one.
fn same<T>(a: T, b: T) -> bool {
    let p = &a;
    let q = &b;
    p == q
}

fn main() {
    if same(5, 5) {
        print("eq");
    } else {
        print("ne");
    }
}
